rvations":{"kolkata":7,"mumbai":7,"evidence":"Both covered by IMD 0.25 deg gridded daily, ERA5 hourly, CHIRPS daily. Mumbai's strong orographic gradient makes 0.25 deg grids less representative; Kolkata's gridded product shows station-sampling artefacts (2025-09-23)."},"satellite_flood_extent":{"kolkata":8,"mumbai":4,"evidence":"Sentinel-1 RTC monsoon (Jun-Oct) acquisitions 2017-2025: Kolkata 523 across 3 orbits, Mumbai 115 on essentially one orbit.","post_hoc":"Later analysis in this run showed Sentinel-1 change detection carries no rainfall-driven signal in Kolkata's built-up cells, so this advantage did not translate into usable urban labels."},"elevation_terrain":{"kolkata":4,"mumbai":7,"evidence":"Copernicus GLO-30 relief (p95-p5): Kolkata 10.1 m, Mumbai 130.2 m. In Kolkata DSM errors are comparable to the terrain signal."},"land_use":{"kolkata":7,"mumbai":7,"evidence":"ESA WorldCover 2021 (10 m) and JRC GSW available for both."},"drainage_infrastructure":{"kolkata":4,"mumbai":5,"evidence":"OSM drain/ditch/canal ways: Kolkata 385, Mumbai 747. Neither city's storm-drain network is openly published."},"population_exposure":{"kolkata":8,"mumbai":6,"evidence":"GHS-POP for both; OSM building ways in study bbox: Kolkata 498,494, Mumbai 116,716; health/school features Kolkata 749, Mumbai 2290."},"independent_validation":{"kolkata":6,"mumbai":7,"evidence":"Kolkata: multi-orbit SAR, IFI, news archive. Mumbai: dense IFI record and larger literature for comparison."}},"weighted_totals_base":{"kolkata":6.55,"mumbai":6.3},"sensitivity":{"historical: locality-level emphasis":{"kolkata":6.8,"mumbai":6.05},"historical: event-level emphasis":{"kolkata":6.3,"mumbai":6.55},"historical: equal (base)":{"kolkata":6.55,"mumbai":6.3},"satellite criterion re-scored post hoc (SAR unusable for urban labels: both 3)":{"kolkata":5.8,"mumbai":6.15}},"selected":"kolkata","decision":"Kolkata selected as pilot. The primary deliverable is a within-city spatial susceptibility map, which needs locality-level observations; these were verified for Kolkata in this run. Totals are close and the ranking flips with reasonable changes to the historical-observation score or the post-hoc satellite score, so the choice is not robustly dominant. Mumbai has the stronger event-level record and is the recommended next city (especially for temporal event modelling).","supervised_event_forecasting_feasible":false,"approach":"Neither city has openly available, systematically recorded, time-stamped urban flood labels at hourly resolution. The pilot therefore uses (A) presence/background susceptibility from documented localities and (B) city-scale documented event-day discrimination from rainfall, not hourly flood forecasting.","evidence_file":"research/city_feasibility_evidence.json"}}